      "text": "Given that the roots of the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + 4x - 11 = 0\\) are \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), by Vieta's formulas, we have:\n\\[\na + b + c = -3,\n\\]\n\\[\nab + bc + ca = 4,\n\\]\n\\[\nabc = 11.\n\\]\nFor the polynomial \\(x^3 + rx^2 + sx + t = 0\\) with roots \\(a+b\\), \\(b+c\\), and \\(c+a\\), by Vieta's formulas, the coefficients are related to the sums and products of these roots. Specifically, the constant term \\(t\\) is given by the product of the roots:\n\\[\nt = (a+b)(b+c)(c+a).\n\\]\nWe can expand this product as follows:\n\\[\n(a+b)(b+c)(c+a) = (a+b+c)((a+b)+(b+c)+(c+a)) - abc.\n\\]\nUsing the values \\(a+b+c = -3\\), \\(ab+bc+ca = 4\\), and \\(abc = 11\\), we substitute these into the expanded form:\n\\[\n(a+b)(b+c)(c+a) = -3(11 + 4) = -3 \\cdot 15 = -45.\n\\]\nTherefore, the value of \\(t\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-45}.\n\\]",
